  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1244 AM MST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nShowers will spread eastward on Saturday as the upper trough moves \nacross southern CO through the day.  The front will back door into \nthe southeast plains bringing cooler temperatures to the plains. \nMoisture appears scant behind the front, but some showers may \nspill off into the adjacent I-25 corridor and southern portions\nof the plains during the afternoon as the upper forcing moves \nthrough. Overall, any precipitation will be light with a dusting\nto up to an inch of snow possible across the southeast \nmountains, and some spotty light precipitation amounts possible\nacross the adjacent plains. \n\nDry weather returns for Saturday night into Sunday, and temperatures \nSunday morning will dip into the teens and single digits for \nthe mountains/valley locations under clear skies. The plains \nwill see lows in the 20s. \n\nSunday will remain dry and cool under shortwave ridging.\n\nAnother fast moving system will move across CO on Monday-Monday \nnight. Similar to the previous system, some wind driven snow\nwill spread into the mountains, and depending on the amplitude \nof the weakening trough as it moves across the plains, we could\nsee some showers spill over into the southeast plains. Overall,\nany precipitation will be brief and light. \n\nDry and cool weather will continue for mid week under another \ntransient upper ridge. Yet another system is progged to move through \naround the Thanksgiving holiday, though it seems more likely to be \nanother open wave producing some light mountain snows according to \nvarious ensemble means.  Operational GFS appears to be the outlier \nsolution with a closed low dropping into the Desert Southwest on \nThursday. Even this solution would keep the majority of the \nprecipitation too far south to affect southern CO.  There's been \nquite a bit of run to run variability over the past couple days with \nthis latter system, keeping the details uncertain.  Cool and \nunsettled weather with light mountain snows seems the most \nlikely scenario for the time being. -KT",
